is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF core is healthy. Every adjacency in the domain — including the two specifically flagged by the ticket (r1↔csw1 on CD_WAN_LINK_1 and r1↔csw2 on CD_WAN_LINK_2) — is in Full state and has been continuously stable since the lab started. FRR's `show ip ospf neighbor detail` reports exactly **6 state changes** on both r1→csw1 and r1→csw2, with the most recent state change logged at startup time. Six state changes is precisely the normal OSPF finite-state machine startup sequence (Down→Init→2-Way→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full counts as 6 transitions), not evidence of any post-convergence bounce. The FRR logs on r1 and csw1 contain only daemon-init messages; there are no neighbor-down or neighbor-up entries after initial convergence. The ospfd processes on both csw1 and csw2 show a start time matching lab boot (13:01), confirming ospfd was never restarted. OSPF Hello/Dead timers are consistent at 10s/40s across all interfaces with no per-interface overrides, no authentication mismatch, and no MTU discrepancy (all interfaces 1500 B). The 12+ SPF runs observed in the first minute are entirely explained by the startup topology churn as six distribution-switch adjacencies converged through the two core switches. The "flapping" alert was a false positive: monitoring systems that watch neighbor state-change events without filtering out the initial startup FSM traversal will see what looks like bouncing. No corrective action is needed — the OSPF core is operating correctly.
=== END ===